{"id":130732,"title":"MPI-hybrid Parallelism for Volume Rendering on Large, Multi-core Systems","url":"https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1012539/?q=parallel volume rendering","final_url":"https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1012539/m2/1/high_res_d/983174.pdf","year":null,"kind":"link","source":"os:digital.library.unt.edu","doi":null,"arxiv":null,"sha256":"192ea12f2d58dd7fcdbf482ef26d9445d31e8f05edf4f43726809a4796de4366","ctype":"application/pdf","text_src":"url","fetched_at":1793305920.8189216,"text_chars":41346,"abstract":"This work studies the performance and scalability characteristics of\"hybrid'\" parallel programming and execution as applied to raycasting volume rendering -- a staple visualization algorithm -- on a large, multi-core platform. Historically, the Message Passing Interface (MPI) has become the de-facto standard for parallel programming and execution on modern parallel systems. As the computing industry trends towards multi-core processors, with four- and six-core chips common today and 128-core chips coming soon, we wish to better understand how algorithmic and parallel programming choices impact performance and scalability on large, distributed-memory multi-core systems. Our findings indicate that the hybrid-parallel implementation, at levels of concurrency ranging from 1,728 to 216,000, performs better, uses a smaller absolute memory footprint, and consumes less communication bandwidth than the traditional, MPI-only implementation.","file":null,"marks":[],"links":["https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1012539/m2/1/high_res_d/983174.pdf","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1012539/?q=parallel volume rendering","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1012539/?q=volume rendering library","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1012539/?q=cuda volume rendering","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1012539/?q=accelerated volume ray casting","https://digital.library